#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:831
msgid "7. Arguments are references"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:833
msgid "A parameter is bound to **the same object** as the argument - Python does not copy it (session 3: names and references). For numbers and strings it makes no difference, because they cannot change. A **list** passed to a function **can** be changed by the function - and the caller sees the change:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:848
msgid "**Function calls step by step:** [5. A list passed to a function](../animations/functionCalls.html#5){target=\"_blank\"}"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:851
msgid "Sometimes that is what you want (a function that fills a list). Usually it is a surprise. The safe default: do not change the arguments - build and **return a new list**:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:868
msgid "8. Docstrings and type hints"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:870
msgid "Docstrings"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:872
msgid "A **docstring** is a string right after the `def` line, in triple quotes. It says what the function does, what it takes and what it returns - for the people who will use it (you, in three months). Tools show it: `help()`, and VS Code when you hover over the name."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:891
msgid "The conventions ([PEP 257](https://peps.python.org/pep-0257/)): the first line is a short sentence that says what the function does; then an empty line and the details."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:894
msgid "Type hints"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:896
msgid "**Type hints** document the types of the parameters and of the result:"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:920
msgid "Hint"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:920
msgid "Means"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:922
msgid "one value of this type"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:923
msgid "a list of floats"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:924
msgid "a tuple of a string and a float"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:925
msgid "a string or `None` (since Python 3.10)"
msgstr ""

#. table cell
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:926
msgid "the function returns nothing"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:928
msgid "Python does **not check** type hints when the code runs - they are documentation:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:937
msgid "But editors and tools do check them: **Pylance** in VS Code underlines a call with a wrong type before you run anything, and [mypy](https://mypy-lang.org/) checks a whole project. In data work, hints are most useful on the functions that other people call."
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:942
msgid "Newer Python versions"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:943
msgid "Python 3.12 added the `type` statement for type aliases: `type Amount = float`. Python 3.14 (the Python of this site) evaluates annotations only when they are needed, so a hint can name a class that is defined later in the file. Neither is needed in this course - the hints above work in 3.12 and 3.13."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:949
msgid "9. Functions are values"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:951
msgid "First-class functions"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:953
msgid "In Python a function is an **object**, like a number or a list. So a function can be:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:955
msgid "bound to another name, or stored in a list or a dictionary;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:956
msgid "passed as an argument to another function;"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:957
msgid "returned as a value from another function."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:969
msgid "A function as an argument"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:986
msgid "A function that takes or returns a function is a **higher-order function**. You already know some: `sorted`, `min` and `max` take a function as `key` (section 10). A report that can use any rounding rule:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1005
msgid "A function that returns a function"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1021
msgid "The inner function **remembers** `n` from the call that made it, even after that call has ended. Such a function is called a **closure**."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1024
msgid "Functions in a dictionary"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1038
msgid "A dictionary of functions replaces a long `if` / `elif` chain: the key chooses the function (a *dispatch table*)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1041
msgid "Pure functions"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1043
msgid "Ideas from **functional programming** that make code easier to trust:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1045
msgid "A **pure function** always returns the same result for the same arguments, and does nothing else: it does not print, does not change its arguments or global variables, does not write files. `expected_loss(pd, lgd, ead)` is pure."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1048
msgid "Anything else a function does is a **side effect**: printing, writing a file, changing a list it got. A program needs side effects - but keep them in a few clear places."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1051
msgid "**Immutable** data (tuples, strings) cannot be changed by mistake."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1053
msgid "Pure functions are easy to test (call, compare the result), easy to reuse, and safe to run again on new data - exactly what a monthly report needs."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1058
msgid "Small functions without a name"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1060
msgid "A **lambda expression** creates a small function without a name, in one line:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1066
msgid "It can have any number of parameters, but only **one expression** - its value is the result. No `return`, no statements."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1092
msgid "Do not give a lambda a name in real code (`double = lambda ...`) - that is what `def` is for, and `def` gives the function a real name in error messages ([PEP 8](https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#programming-recommendations)). A lambda is for a function you need **once, right here** - most often as the `key` of a sort."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1097
msgid "Sorting by a key"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1099
msgid "`sorted()` and `list.sort()` take a `key`: a function that is called for each item, and the items are sorted by its result. This is `ORDER BY` with any expression:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1123
msgid "`key` also works with `min` and `max` - the loan with the largest outstanding amount, not only the amount:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1132
msgid "A list of dictionaries (session 6), sorted by one key:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1146
msgid "A named function works as a key too - `key=len`, `key=str.lower`:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1154
msgid "Sorting is covered in depth in session 5. Reference: [Sorting techniques](https://docs.python.org/3/howto/sorting.html)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1157
msgid "11. Decorators"
msgstr ""

#. box title
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1159
msgid "Advanced"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1160
msgid "Decorators are not needed for the tasks of this course, but you will meet them in libraries (`@property` and `@dataclass` in session 11, `@pytest.fixture`, `@app.get` in web frameworks). This section explains the `@` you will see."
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1165
msgid "The idea"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1167
msgid "A **decorator** is a function that takes a function and returns a new, \"decorated\" function: it adds something **before and after** the original, without changing its code. `decorated = decorator(original)`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1186
msgid "The `@` syntax"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1188
msgid "`@decorator` above a `def` is a short way to write `name = decorator(name)`:"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1205
msgid "Arguments and the return value"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1207
msgid "The wrapper must pass the arguments on, and return the result of the original. `*args, **kwargs` (section 4) take any arguments; `functools.wraps` keeps the name and the docstring of the original. A useful decorator - how long each step of a report takes:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1237
msgid "Read more: [Primer on Python decorators](https://realpython.com/primer-on-python-decorators/) (Real Python)."
msgstr ""

#. heading 2
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1240
msgid "12. From macros to functions: a small library"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1242
msgid "One task, many small functions"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1244
msgid "The portfolio rules of sessions 1 and 2, as functions. Each one does one thing, has a docstring, and returns its result:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1285
msgid "A function can call other functions - each call gets its own local names, and returns to the line that called it:"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1289
msgid "**Function calls step by step:** [4. A function calls a function](../animations/functionCalls.html#4){target=\"_blank\"}"
msgstr ""

#. heading 3
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1292
msgid "A script made of functions"
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1294
msgid "The structure of a script that you can run again every month: the imports and the constants at the top, then the functions, and the main code at the bottom."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1346
msgid "Compare it with the first example of session 2: the same result, but now each part has a name, can be tested alone, and can be reused - `read_loans` in the next report, `dpd_bucket` in a check of the data."
msgstr ""

#. paragraph
#: site/lectures/04-functions.md:1350
msgid "In a file that is run as a script, the main code is often put under `if __name__ == \"__main__\":` - so that the functions can be imported by another file without running the report. Modules and `import` of your own files are the topic of session 10."
msgstr ""

